1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,950
The Company That Outlived
Its Own Tin Smelter

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:07,395
By 1912, a company most
Singaporeans today have

3
00:00:07,475 --> 00:00:12,120
never heard of was smelting
roughly a third of the world's tin,

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,125
on a small island just off the harbour.

5
00:00:15,125 --> 00:00:18,941
Its ingots, stamped "Straits
Tin," were internationally

6
00:00:19,021 --> 00:00:22,350
recognised as the purest
grade money could buy.

7
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,416
The smelter that made
that possible is gone,

8
00:00:25,496 --> 00:00:29,349
the island it stood on is a
naval base, and the company

9
00:00:29,429 --> 00:00:33,075
itself doesn't smelt anything
in Singapore anymore.

10
00:00:33,075 --> 00:00:37,920
It is, remarkably, still around,
still listed, still making money,

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,775
more than a century after the
furnaces on Pulau Brani first lit.

12
00:00:42,775 --> 00:00:46,734
The company was called the Straits
Trading Company, and it was

13
00:00:46,814 --> 00:00:50,512
incorporated in Singapore on
the 8th of November 1887 with

14
00:00:50,592 --> 00:00:53,965
$150,000 in capital, the
work of an unlikely pairing:

15
00:00:54,045 --> 00:00:57,808
Herman Muhlinghaus, a German
entrepreneur, and James Sword,

16
00:00:57,888 --> 00:01:02,238
a Scottish businessman from the
merchant house Gilfillan, Wood &amp;

17
00:01:02,318 --> 00:01:06,016
Co. They started modestly,
with five small coastal vessels

18
00:01:06,096 --> 00:01:10,200
running trade routes rather than
any grand industrial ambition.

19
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,615
The pivot came in 1890, when
they opened their first smelting

20
00:01:14,695 --> 00:01:19,479
plant on Pulau Brani, a small
island just off Singapore's harbour,

21
00:01:19,559 --> 00:01:22,875
built on the site of an
old, unused dry dock.

22
00:01:22,875 --> 00:01:26,901
The idea was straightforward
but not obvious at the time:

23
00:01:26,981 --> 00:01:31,224
instead of leaving tin ore to
be smelted in small batches by

24
00:01:31,304 --> 00:01:36,267
traditional Chinese-run furnaces
scattered across the Malay Peninsula,

25
00:01:36,347 --> 00:01:40,525
centralise it into one large,
coal-fired, efficient works.

26
00:01:40,525 --> 00:01:41,975
It worked.

27
00:01:41,975 --> 00:01:45,996
Pulau Brani's furnaces could
process ore at a scale and

28
00:01:46,076 --> 00:01:50,625
consistency the old scattered
smelters simply couldn't match.

29
00:01:50,625 --> 00:01:55,778
A second plant followed in 1902, at
Butterworth in Province Wellesley,

30
00:01:55,858 --> 00:01:58,250
on the mainland opposite Penang.

31
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:01,221
Between the two works, the company's tin,

32
00:02:01,301 --> 00:02:06,133
branded "Straits Tin" and shipped
as 100-pound ingots stamped with

33
00:02:06,213 --> 00:02:10,376
the company's own name, became
internationally recognised

34
00:02:10,456 --> 00:02:14,400
by the 1900s as the purest
grade of tin in the world.

35
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,754
By 1912, Malaya was
producing roughly half the

36
00:02:17,834 --> 00:02:21,637
world's supply of tin, and
two-thirds of that output

37
00:02:21,717 --> 00:02:25,375
was refined by the Straits
Trading Company alone.

38
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:29,081
A single firm, working
through two coal-fired plants

39
00:02:29,161 --> 00:02:32,139
a strait apart, had become one of the most

40
00:02:32,219 --> 00:02:36,150
important players in a global
industrial supply chain.

41
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:38,621
The Pulau Brani smelter's biggest

42
00:02:38,701 --> 00:02:42,025
disruption didn't come
from a market crash.

43
00:02:42,025 --> 00:02:44,850
It came from the British themselves.

44
00:02:44,850 --> 00:02:49,877
In 1941, with the Japanese invasion
of Malaya already underway,

45
00:02:49,957 --> 00:02:54,822
the smelting plant on Pulau
Brani was deliberately destroyed,

46
00:02:54,902 --> 00:03:01,225
denying it to the advancing enemy just
before Singapore fell in February 1942.

47
00:03:01,225 --> 00:03:05,350
It was scorched-earth policy
applied to a tin smelter.

48
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:09,915
Staff began returning to the
ruined works in December 1945,

49
00:03:09,995 --> 00:03:15,033
and rebuilding started under the
company's long-serving chairman,

50
00:03:15,113 --> 00:03:18,261
Sir John Bagnall, who had led the Straits

51
00:03:18,341 --> 00:03:22,750
Trading Company since 1923
and would stay on until 1947.

52
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:28,091
Smelting resumed at Pulau Brani within
roughly a year of the staff's return,

53
00:03:28,171 --> 00:03:31,871
even as the postwar tin
market itself faced a slower,

54
00:03:31,951 --> 00:03:35,295
structural threat: cheap
synthetic alternatives,

55
00:03:35,375 --> 00:03:40,725
aluminium chief among them, had begun
eating into demand for tin worldwide.

56
00:03:40,725 --> 00:03:46,050
Pulau Brani's smelter outlasted
the war by only two more decades.

57
00:03:46,050 --> 00:03:50,839
In the late 1960s, the plant
moved off the island entirely,

58
00:03:50,919 --> 00:03:55,461
making way for what became
Singapore's first naval base,

59
00:03:55,541 --> 00:03:58,925
officially opened on Pulau Brani in 1974.

60
00:03:58,925 --> 00:04:02,246
The furnaces that had once
made Singapore synonymous

61
00:04:02,326 --> 00:04:05,254
with the world's purest
tin were gone from the

62
00:04:05,334 --> 00:04:08,800
island for good, after more
than seventy years there.

63
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:12,850
The Butterworth plant on the
mainland kept going longer.

64
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:18,101
In 1982, it was folded into the
Malaysia Smelting Corporation Berhad,

65
00:04:18,181 --> 00:04:20,650
a separate company that took over

66
00:04:20,730 --> 00:04:24,825
the group's actual smelting
operations going forward.

67
00:04:24,825 --> 00:04:28,804
Tin smelting, the business
the Straits Trading Company

68
00:04:28,884 --> 00:04:32,111
had been built around
in 1887, was no longer

69
00:04:32,191 --> 00:04:36,325
something the company did
directly in Singapore at all.

70
00:04:36,325 --> 00:04:40,225
What replaced tin, gradually,
was everything else.

71
00:04:40,225 --> 00:04:43,550
Through the late twentieth
century the company

72
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:48,139
diversified into property
management and financial investment,

73
00:04:48,219 --> 00:04:51,617
and in 1997 it launched
a hospitality arm under

74
00:04:51,697 --> 00:04:55,250
the Rendezvous Hotels
name, still running today.

75
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:59,027
In 2008, the Tecity Group
acquired a majority stake,

76
00:04:59,107 --> 00:05:02,661
and the group that emerged
looks nothing like the

77
00:05:02,741 --> 00:05:06,375
smelting operation
Muhlinghaus and Sword founded.

78
00:05:06,375 --> 00:05:10,962
Today the Straits Trading Company
describes itself as a conglomerate

79
00:05:11,042 --> 00:05:15,355
across three divisions: Resources,
meaning a 73 percent stake in

80
00:05:15,435 --> 00:05:20,915
the Malaysia Smelting Corporation, now
listed in its own right on Bursa Malaysia;

81
00:05:20,995 --> 00:05:24,484
Property, spanning commercial
and retail real estate

82
00:05:24,564 --> 00:05:29,300
across Asia and Europe; and Hospitality,
the Rendezvous Hotels chain.

83
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:33,858
The company remains listed
on the Singapore Exchange,

84
00:05:33,938 --> 00:05:38,145
reporting a net income of
218.4 million Singapore

85
00:05:38,225 --> 00:05:43,125
dollars in 2025, under Executive
Chairman Chew Gek Khim.

86
00:05:43,125 --> 00:05:47,233
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Singapore's founding fortunes

87
00:05:47,313 --> 00:05:52,011
are usually told through trade and
entrepot commerce, not heavy industry,

88
00:05:52,091 --> 00:05:55,937
but for a few decades the Straits
Trading Company ran one of

89
00:05:56,017 --> 00:06:00,322
the most important industrial
monopolies of the colonial world from

90
00:06:00,402 --> 00:06:04,525
a small island most Singaporeans
today couldn't place on a map.

91
00:06:04,525 --> 00:06:08,766
It survived a self-inflicted
wartime demolition, lost the island

92
00:06:08,846 --> 00:06:13,830
itself to the navy, spun its own core
business off into a separate company,

93
00:06:13,910 --> 00:06:17,071
and turned into something
almost unrecognisable,

94
00:06:17,151 --> 00:06:22,350
a property-and-hotels conglomerate that
happens to still hold a stake in tin.

95
00:06:22,350 --> 00:06:26,962
Few nineteenth-century Singapore
firms can say they're still

96
00:06:27,042 --> 00:06:32,125
trading in 2026 at all, let alone
after changing this completely.
